{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And he took up his parable, etc. The utterance of Balaam, etc. and the utterance, etc.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to say <b>and he took up his parable.</b> Further, what need is there to say <b>the utterance of Balaam</b> — it is obvious that it is his mouth speaking? Further, why was it necessary to inform us of his father's name here? Further, why was it necessary to identify himself with signs of his body? Also, in the phrase ״who hears,״ etc. — what was intended by this, and if it is to praise himself, why did he not praise himself the first time?" "Indeed, since until now he had been speaking whatever HaShem placed in his mouth against his will — the speech issuing from his mouth involuntarily — and now he wished to give his own assent to the words issuing from his mouth as if he were saying them of his own simple volition. And his intention in this was also that if he were to curse, the force of blessing would come and bless instead, but if he were to bless, the force of blessing would not bless. And moreover, according to what I explained in the verse ״and he set his face toward the wilderness״ etc. in the second interpretation — that he intended to prepare himself to bless Israel — the verse is well understood in this manner: <b>and he took up his parable</b> — meaning, the parable that is from himself and from his own knowledge. And with this the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 105b) are reconciled, for they said that he said ״like streams״ etc. — he sought to disparage them as a stream that dries up in the summer, etc., but the holy spirit countered him: ״No, not as you think, but נִטָּיו״ — meaning, like a river that never ceases but flows continually. ״Like tents״ — meaning that they are tossed about and uprooted from their place, and the holy spirit countered him ״HaShem planted״ etc. And so all his words are blessings whose inner content is a curse, and if they are words of the holy spirit, why would it say something containing a flaw? Rather, they are certainly his own words." "<b>And therefore</b> he said <b>and he took up his parable and said: The utterance of Balaam</b> — meaning, these words are my parable and are not like the preceding words that did not come from me, and he identified who is speaking these words so that it would be recorded for all time who the author is. And he gave three signs, in the manner stated in the Mishnah (Pe'ah 7:1): every olive tree that has a name, etc. — by its name, by its deeds, and by its place. Corresponding to ״its name״ he said <b>the son of Beor</b>, and he said ״his son״ and did not say ״a son of״ — to say that his father was beneath him and it was fitting that he be in the rank of his son relative to the level of apprehension. Or it may be said that Beor had no one but Balaam, therefore he said ״his son״ — meaning, Beor's only one. And should you find that he had others, he said that all of them are accounted as nothing before him as though he has none but Balaam. Corresponding to ״its deeds״ he said <b>the man whose eye is shut</b> (שְׁתֻם הָעָיִן), for it was recognizable by the deed of his eyes — wherever he would look with his eyes, he would inflict harm, and therefore he would shut his evil eye so as not to cause harm. And it is possible that his left eye was specially designated for this. And if we interpret שְׁתֻם הָעָיִן in the sense of opening, it intends that he was marked by the opening of his eye to do harm. Corresponding to ״its place״ he said <b>who hears the words of El</b>, for he stood as a banner for the nations, and no other man stood in this place of prophecy." "<b>Further,</b> it intends to raise the question of why he said ״utterance״ (נְאֻם) three times and was not satisfied with one. Indeed, since there are in Balaam three gradations of hidden forces: first — a force that comes through his creation, which the one who begot him produced in him; second — what he acquired through the power of his deeds, for a person draws to himself forces according to what is activated within him; third — what HaShem bestowed upon him for a known reason, to be a prophet to the nations, so that the nations would not complain, as stated in their words of blessed memory (Numbers Rabbah 20:1). And Balaam intended to speak his words with the agreement of all his forces together: corresponding to the force that comes from the power of the one who begot him, he said <b>the utterance of Balaam the son of Beor</b>, and he was precise to say ״his son״ and did not say ״a son of,״ for then it would have been heard as identifying who begot him, whereas he intended to speak of the portion within him deriving from the fact that he is his son, and not of what resides in his power this day; and corresponding to the portion he acquired through his own power he said <b>the utterance of the man</b> (הַגֶּבֶר) — meaning, the man, what he acquired by being a mighty man (גֶּבֶר) of himself; also hinting at his prowess from the language of גְּבוּרָה — <b>whose eye is shut</b> (שְׁתֻם הָעָיִן), that he opened his eyes, as they said of blessed memory (Zohar vol. III 206a) that he used to go to Uzza and Azael and there acquired forces of impurity, and his eyes were opened to hidden and wondrous things after many exertions, as stated in their words: how many exertions are required for this thing; and corresponding to the force within him that HaShem bestowed for the known reason he said <b>the utterance of him who hears the words of El</b>, and he doubled, saying <b>the words of El, and the vision</b> etc., corresponding to the two aspects of apprehension: one — when the speech came to his ears, and the second — when it was revealed to him. And corresponding to both of them he said <b>fallen, with eyes unveiled</b> — meaning, when he would behold a vision of Shaddai he would fall, and when the speech would come to him and he would hear it in his ears, he would not fall but rather his eyes would be unveiled."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:13:57.335476003Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}